We'll sort intervals by start. Then compress ends. Iterate with BIT.

Edge Cases: When multiple intervals have same start, the BIT query for current interval will count previous intervals that intersect. Since intersection includes endpoints, condition is previous end >= current start. That's exactly what we count with `end >= start`. In BIT, we count intervals with end < start as those not intersecting. So we subtract those from total previous.

